A||||18||Will||MR2 question for PearlBlueSoul, ArmandoP, etc.||||1||
Z||000000||Will||11-30-2000||07:14 PM||||What engine swaps are popular with MR2's?<P>Along the lines of a Northstar swap, I was wondering if a Lexus V8 would fit. Could it be used with a Celica 6 spd transaxle? Could an VVT-i V6 (Avalon) fit?<P>For that matter, has anyone swapped the new Celica transaxle and/or engine into their cars?<P>What's available for suspension/brakes?<BR>How large can you go with wheels/tires?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cheever2||11-30-2000||07:25 PM||||Have MR2's been rebodied (i.e. kitcars)?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||11-30-2000||07:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever2:<BR><B>Have MR2's been rebodied (i.e. kitcars)?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I remember seeing a F355 kit for the MR2, a couple of years ago.. not sure where though.. and I haven't heard anything about them since.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000003||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||08:56 PM||||OK, to answer this you have to know things about years and japan. <P>In japan, the Supercharged MR2s came out in 1986, whereas here they came out in 1988.  Also in japan, they transferred over to MAP based sensors versus the AFM units we use over here, and gained more HP.  Japan spec S/C swaps are very common, and a US spec S/C swap is basically a drop in affair.  <P>What I had considered doing is taking the motor from a late 80's corolla (known as the AE92) and putting that in my car.  Reason?  The Corollas (both AE86 and AE92) used the same engine as the MR2 in both transverse and longitudinal mounting configurations (front or rear drive, FX16 or GT-S respectively).  In the AE92 however, they decided to make the intake ports smaller and get rid of a system toyota calls TVIS, or Toyota Variable Intake System. This system is composed of 2 intake runners per cylinder, with the secondary set coming open around 4500 RPM by a vaccum actuated valve.  <P>With the headwork they did, new injectors and computer, and adding an oil squirter for the crank for cooling at high RPM, they successfully upped the HP to around 140 at the flywheel (if I remember right).  Normal 4AGs (as the engine is commonly referred to, it's toyota's engine code) only make about 120 HP at the flywheel, which translates on my MR2 to around 97 HP in stock trim.  with my intake, I managed to break open into 100 HP territory, spinnin the rollers to 101.4 HP.  <P>Another possible engine swap is a RARE motor known as the 4AG 20 valve.  In these motors, toyota went with what is now called VVT or Variable Valve Timing (similar to Vtech), and added one more intake valve per cylinder totaling 5 valves per cylinder.   With this engine, toyota managed to push the envelope on power to the tune of 170 HP, this on the Same block, just different heads.  Also, this motor had independant quad throttle bodies, and a host of other goodies.  Trouble is, they only came in corollas, and hence are very large.  It has been done with a battery relocation, and relocations of otehr types, but it's very rare to see one 'stuffed' in the back of MR2s.  And also the motor's price lends itself to being very hard to get.<P>And the last conversion that's recently taken over the MKI scene is the V6 Camry conversion, in either Turbo or TRD Supercharged form.  With this conversion, you take a V6 from a camry/solara and literally stuff it in the back with a Camry 5 speed.  Not neglecting of course to get the TRD supercharger.  There are links on Atlanta MR2s.com to someone who helped to start the v6 craze in the MR2 community.  <P>That's it as far as I know.  There are other conversions such as the SARD v8 but that's for the MKII MR2, and also 3SGTE (MR2 turbo motor) conversions have been *discussed* but I don't believe ever done. <P>As far as Ferrari conversions go, there are only 2 that I know of, both by erebuni.  Here are the pics to those:  <P>355:   <IMG SRC="http://www.erebunicorp.com/images/autos2/mr2028f.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.erebunicorp.com/images/autos2/mr2028r.jpg"> <P>348  <IMG SRC="http://www.erebunicorp.com/images/autos1/MR2166f.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.erebunicorp.com/images/autos1/MR2166r.jpg"> <P><BR>Whew, there's more, but I'll continue onto another post here in a sec.<P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jake||11-30-2000||09:05 PM||||I must say, I'm quite partial to that 348||||reg||1
Z||000005||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||09:11 PM||||Here's some other senelsess garble I just thought I'd mention.  it really is just little facts and figures that i remember, and thought were kinda noteworthy.<P><BR>Toyota has made many versions of the 4AG for racing, ranging from 240 Naturally aspirated horsepower, to 700HP in turbo form on the same block.  the naturally aspirated varieties tend to spin to a max RPM of around 12k, and I would think it would be the same with the turbo versions.  in stock trim for consumer use, the 4ag would rev out to a maximum of 9 thousand RPMs, with the accessories giving out around 8.  So, they have a redline at 7500 RPM.  But, These motors are highly modifiable, and have had very successful lives in racing and in modified forms.  <P>With various forms of modifications around ranging from Supercharger upgrades, stroker kits, Dual charge (Turbo and supercharge) upgrades, intercooler upgrades, turbo upgrades, pistons, rods, headwork (alot of that), lightened flywheels, cams, cam gears, ignition, and a host of other parts IMHO make this one of the most tunable japanese motors out there.  Red hat (AE92 corolla) japan spec motors price out at around 450 bucks, so these motors are also very cheep.  Most people rather replace the motor than rebuild it.  <P>A slight word on the supercharged motors:  The 4AGZE as it is known, has a few notable modifications that are farily inexpensive to do that yield great results in boost.  For starters, I believe the stock, roots type blower makes around 8 psi, and is known as the SC13.  With the SC14 upgrade, you push around 10-15psi.  Then, there are overdrive pulleys made by various companies that will bring up the boost even more to near 20 psi.  Little modifications to the motor like this yeild decent results, and I belive it's been brought up to 150-175 hp.  The stock motor runs at 140 from what I know, and thse are at the wheel figures.  The 4AGZE is stable up to 200 hp before internal modifications are neccessary.  <P>That's about it.  These engine swaps and internal upgrades are all for the first generation MR2.  I don't know very much about the second generations, so perhaps someone else from the board could come over and fill that gap.<BR><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000006||wkayl||11-30-2000||09:23 PM||||Now here is a guy that knows his car....||||reg||1
Z||000007||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||09:24 PM||||I didn't see your other questions up there!  wups, sorry bout that.<P>As far as using a v8 lexus motor, yes this has been done by a company known as SARD.  with some tweaking of the bodywork, they did manage to stuff in a lexus v8 into the back of a MKII MR2.  <P>With wheels, on a MKI MR2 you don't need to go any bigger than 16, but there are quite a few with 17" wheels.  Only trouble with these wheels is that they tend to wear out steering and driveline components like wheel hubs, racks, and other things farily quickly.  my car actually had 17" cragar 5 stars on it when I got it, and it rode horribly (bent wheels tho, I've ridden in MR2s with 17's and they run fine).  When I put the 14" momos from my old MR2 onto my 87, it made a world of difference, and showed how badly the rack had been worn out by the 17s.  Also, the brakes were heat glazed from having to stop the cragars (which weighed near or over 25lbs a piece!  think of that in terms of inertial mass, and that's intensely heavy!)  the only advantage to large wheels that I see is that they can hold their inertia better at high speeds allowing you to go faster.  on MKII MR2s, 18" sizes are not at all uncommon, and using deep dish wheels is especially tasteful on those cars with the appropriate wide body kits from TRD, or any wide body kit in general.<P>As far as brake kits go, there are kits out there for MR2s like mine by the presidents of the MR2 club here in atlanta that have 11 inch rotors, and quad piston calipers.  I must say, these conversions are both beautiful and VERY functional in their design, using rotors from Nascar and also from one other form of racing (I forgot!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> ).  <P>As far as celica transplants go, in MKII MR2s you must remember that a Celica is in a sense a 4wd MR2.  The MR2 and the celica's motor are the exact same.  No one transplants celica motors into MKII MR2s, but n/a MKII owners sometimes will do MKII motor swaps, and even the turbo guys like to use the higher output version motors from Japan and import those.  MKII MR2s are VERY upgradable, and have the greatest HP yields of any car I've seen (far side of a supra).  in japan, these 4 cylinder motors fully built are easily achieving 600 HP.  The Supras are verging on Evil with numbers in the thousands.  So, to say that a MKII owner would put his money in a celica is a little odd when he can get a MKII motor that is the exact same thing, probably a tad cheeper.<P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000008||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||09:33 PM||||here are some pics of the Lexus V8 MR2: <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.mr2.com/TEXT/GRAPHICS/sard/mc88.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.mr2.com/TEXT/GRAPHICS/sard/mc8r.jpg">  <P>And here's some of Toyota's Group B rally car, the 700HP 4AGE and 4wd MR2 MKI.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.mr2.com/ARTICLE/PICTURES/700hpMkI/700hp.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.mr2.com/ARTICLE/PICTURES/700hpMkI/MRprotoXfront.gif">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.mr2.com/ARTICLE/PICTURES/700hpMkI/MRprotoXrear.gif">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.mr2.com/ARTICLE/PICTURES/700hpMkI/MRprotoXcockpit.gif">  <P><BR>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">        <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.<p>[This message has been edited by PearlBlueSoul (edited 11-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||09:40 PM||||AND LAST BUT NOT LEAST, suspension.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  this is where I shine alot.  hehe.<P>I posted this in the O/T forum, but figured I'd post it here too. <P>I actually didn't know that you guys had double wishbone. I envy you now intensley! hehe. That setup actually INCREASES negative camber in hard cornering, whereas our setup increases positive camber.<BR>About the toe issue, in the rear it is designed to help combat wheelspin. When you accelerate, that 'squat' actually causes the rear tires to bite, allowing you to leave the line quicker. I've only been able to chirp the tires in my n/A in first gear, even when I revved up to where my max torque is at 4500 (88.4 lb ft). <P>The design of the suspension is a coil over type Macpherson setup all around. Coils on top over a macpherson strut casing, with the original struts being rebuildable units from the factory. You also can get inserts to fit in the stock sized strut casings, and I know Tokico, and KYB both make adjustable units for replacement (via a knob at the top of the strut. manipulates internal shock valving and baffles to stiffen the strut). <P>The setup on MR2s is quite simple. I know of only a few things you can do to MR2s for the suspension, whereas I know of about 10 things for an Integra that you could do in the way of suspension stuff. if I ever really wanted to go all out on handling, this is what I would get. <P>HKS Adjustable pillow ball upper strut hats- Allows for the tuning of the camber via adjustments at the top of the strut. <P>Suspension Techniques adjustable sway bar kit- This kit allows tuning of the sway bar, via different holes drilled into the ends of the bar itself. Can be tuned for understeer, oversteer, or neutral handling. Comes with polyurethane bushings and adjustable rear end links.<P>Ground Control adjustable spring conversion (slip and weld-on kit)- for adjustments of the spring rates and ride height. For a drop greater than 2" with this kit, you must get the weld on kit that GC sells. <P>Tokico Illumina 5 way adjustable struts- this long word basically means you can set the dampening rate between 5 settings, raning from softer to harder. I would more than likely run it a tad stiffer in the back to help combat the extra weight imposed by the motor and to combat squat on acceleration.<P>Strut tower brace: Helps to maintain chassis rigidity during a corner. The struts will actually 'bend' in cornering, causing un-needed chassis flex and a waste of weight distribution during. This brace helps to lock in the tops of the struts to maintain their original geometry, thereby allowing the suspension to put more load to the tires and increase the friction circle the tires have on the road.<P>*whew*! hehe. If you've ever looked at my style of posting, I do this quite often when people ask me, "what is that?". Just thought I'd give you a taste of how I post on tech topics. <P>Hope this helps some.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||11-30-2000||10:28 PM||||Don't worry, I can handle it!<P>When I mentioned Celicas, I was mostly thinking of the 6 spd transaxle in the brand new ones (What are they up to now, 7th gen?), which are FWD as I understand it. I've looked up the ratios and it looks like a nice closely spaced box good for a high revving motor (Obviously! it's used with one). I've been a little curious about how much torque it can stand.<BR>     How are Toyota transmissions/transaxles in general? Are they usually good for much more than stock power?<P>     I'm looking into a using 6 spd converted VW 02A transaxle in my car. I want a very tall sixth for good highway mileage. The kit from <A HREF="http://www.stealthracing.co.uk" TARGET=_blank>www.stealthracing.co.uk</A>  is good for 400++ ftlbs. <A HREF="http://www.quaifeamerica.com" TARGET=_blank>www.quaifeamerica.com</A>  has a kit also, but it's a very close ratio box with 6th shorter than stock 5th.<BR>     Soon you'll be even more envious of Fiero suspension. I'm designing a SLA conversion kit for the rear suspension.<BR>     When you said the wheels wore out the rack, did you mean that moving all that extra weight around is what did it? That's pretty wild.<P><BR>Good to have you! Keep the info flowing!||||reg||1
Z||000011||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||10:36 PM||||well, we've discussed this on the MR2 board, and reached the conclusion we didn't think it could work.  You could try getting the jap spec 6 speed from a Levin corolla, those bolt up to 4AGs and may work on an application like yours.  post your query on the MR2 board!  I'm sure they'd be happy to answer it!  <P>[URL]http://board.mr2pages.com] MR2pages BBS [/URL]<P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000012||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||10:39 PM||||About the wheels, yah.  Think of all that added inertia from a set of larger wheels!  this is why getting forged lightweight wheels like from Rayes Engineering are so important, because they reduce that mass by a bunch.  I forget the formula, but rotational mass is much heavier than stationary mass.  Think of it this way:  what would you weigh on a planet that had about 3-4 times our gravity??<P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
